{
	title: 'Address saved outputs and images by history item',
	spec: 'Saved tool output is recoverable through the existing session/history-item address, session/n and session/n.i, not only an opaque random blob ID. Item numbering matches history links: first item n, subsequent items n.1, n.2; tool result images occupy subsequent slots. READ_BLOB accepts these addresses and existing session#kindN references with optional item suffixes, recovers complete saved output rather than its capped preview, and reads individual image slots. Capped tool results persist an optional outputBlob link and advertise their durable history address once recorded. Legacy blob IDs, image upload names, single-image histories, old capped-result footers and historical record-level reads stay readable. Pruning recovery markers use stable item addresses for saved outputs and images. Validate new metadata and address numbers, preserve complete errors and paging/character recovery, and report dropped rebase records. Storage remains session-owned; addresses require no growing alias index or duplicate bytes. Focused behavior tests verify multi-result image slot alignment, full output recovery after disk reread, cross-session references, legacy recovery, paging and invalid/missing references.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Existing n.i links enumerate displayed items, not blob ordinal alone: a tool result consumes one slot and each attached image consumes the following slots. Stable references resolve through durable history to session-owned bytes; opaque physical file names remain storage details, avoiding byte copies or an ever-growing alias registry. An optional outputBlob link allows full recovery without parsing newly generated preview text; legacy previews retain a footer fallback. Unattached uploads keep their existing staging names, and replay-only thinking signatures keep internal signatureBlob links.',
		'Invariant: replacing a tool result through rebase clears outputBlob and both image fields. A stable history address must recover the replacement text, never resurrect the original full-output blob or its former image slots. This applies to direct result edits and edits addressed through the paired tool call.',
		'All-numeric legacy six- or twelve-character blob IDs can resemble a history number. An existing legacy blob keeps precedence for slash references; the hash form forces history lookup. Item suffixes are unambiguous. Recovery scans history as before and adds no retained index; appends and pruning count slots once per record.',
		'Cap reserves enough preview room for the longer durable address substituted at append. A zero-character tail budget must omit the tail: slice(-0) returns the entire string and can expose unbounded output. Rebase-agent replacement reports also clear outputBlob. No byte migration, new dependency, background scan, or retained alias registry is required.'
